\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly two of the lines are sunny (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n  the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nDenote by \\(S\\) the set of the two sunny lines and by \\(N\\) the single\nnon\u2011sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nA line that contains three points of \\(T_{3}\\) must be one of the\nfollowing:\n\n* \\(x=1\\) (vertical, parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(y=1\\) (horizontal, parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(x+y=4\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll three are non\u2011sunny by definition.  \nHence any **sunny** line can contain **at most two** points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Each line must contain exactly two points\n\nSince we have three lines covering the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) and each\nline can cover at most two points, the only way to cover all six points\nis that **each line contains exactly two points** (over\u2011covering would\nrequire a line to contain three points, which would be non\u2011sunny, but we\nalready have only one non\u2011sunny line).\n\nThus the three lines partition the six points into three disjoint pairs.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The non\u2011sunny line must be one of the three forbidden directions\n\nThe non\u2011sunny line \\(N\\) must contain at least two points of \\(T_{3}\\);\notherwise the two sunny lines would have to cover all six points, which\nis impossible because each sunny line can cover at most two points.\nConsequently \\(N\\) must be one of the three lines that contain at least\ntwo points of \\(T_{3}\\) and are parallel to a forbidden direction:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N\\in\\{\\,x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=4\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nWe treat the three possibilities separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Examination of the three possibilities\n\n---\n\n#### (a)  \\(N\\) is the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n\\(N\\) contains the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny pair of points from \\(R\\) determines a line as follows:\n\n* \\((2,1)-(2,2)\\): vertical line \\(x=2\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, non\u2011sunny);\n* \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\): horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, non\u2011sunny);\n* \\((2,2)-(3,1)\\): line \\(x+y=4\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), non\u2011sunny).\n\nThus **no** pair of points of \\(R\\) lies on a sunny line.  \nEach sunny line can therefore contain at most one point of \\(R\\), so the\ntwo sunny lines together can cover at most two points of \\(R\\), leaving\nat least one point of \\(R\\) uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### (b)  \\(N\\) is the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n\\(N\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nPairs from \\(R\\):\n\n* \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\): vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\): horizontal line \\(y=2\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, non\u2011sunny);\n* \\((1,3)-(2,2)\\): line \\(x+y=4\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n\nAgain no pair of points of \\(R\\) lies on a sunny line, so the two sunny\nlines can cover at most two points of \\(R\\), leaving one uncovered \u2013 a\ncontradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### (c)  \\(N\\) is the line \\(x+y=4\\).\n\n\\(N\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nPairs from \\(R\\):\n\n* \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\): vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\): horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\): line \\(x+y=3\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), non\u2011sunny).\n\nThus, as before, no pair of points of \\(R\\) can be placed on a sunny\nline, and the two sunny lines can cover at most two points of \\(R\\),\nleaving one uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll three possible choices for the unique non\u2011sunny line lead to a\ncontradiction with the requirement that the two sunny lines cover the\nremaining points. Therefore a configuration of three distinct lines that\ncovers all points of \\(T_{3}\\) while having exactly two sunny lines cannot\nexist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines does not exist.}}\n\\]"
