ceil n/q\\rceil\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such\nabscissas, which yields (1).  Consequently a sunny line contains at most\n\\(M\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  Then the total number of covered\npoints is at most \\(nM\\).  Using (1),\n\n\\[\nnM\\le n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\na contradiction.  Hence a configuration with \\(k=n\\) does **not** exist.\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\)\nand containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{Lines (sunny in bold)}\\\\ \\hline\n0 & x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\\\\n1 & \\mathbf{y=x},\\;x=1,\\;y=1\\\\\n2 & \\mathbf{y=x},\\;\\mathbf{y=x+1},\\;y=1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nEach family consists of three distinct lines and covers the six points\nof \\(T_3\\).  Thus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge3\\); i.e. for every\n\\(0\\le k\\le m-1\\) there is a set \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct\nlines covering \\(T_m\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nLet \\(n=m+1\\) and let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\nIf \\(k\\le m-1\\) we construct a configuration for \\((n,k)\\) as follows.\n\n*Add the horizontal line*  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;y=1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny.  Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) by the\nvector \\((0,1)\\); denote the translated family by\n\\(\\mathcal L'_{m,k}\\).  The translation sends the set \\(T_m\\) onto\n\n\\[\nT'_m:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_{m+1}\\mid b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently the \\(m\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,k}\\) together with\n\\(L_0\\) cover all points of \\(T_{m+1}\\).  The number of sunny lines in\n\\(\\mathcal L'_{m,k}\\) is the same as in \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\), namely \\(k\\);\n\\(L_0\\) is non‑sunny, so the total number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct because \\(L_0\\) has ordinate \\(1\\) whereas every\nline of \\(\\mathcal L'_{m,k}\\) has ordinate at least \\(2\\) at some point.\nThus we obtain a valid configuration for \\((n,k)\\).\n\nIf \\(k=m\\) (the only remaining possibility, since \\(k\\le n-1=m\\)),\ntake the configuration for \\((m,m-1)\\) supplied by the induction\nhypothesis and replace the added horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by a sunny line\nthat passes through the point \\((1,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=x\\)).  This\nincreases the number of sunny lines by one, giving exactly \\(k=m\\).\n\nHence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration\nexists for \\(n=m+1\\).\n\nBy induction the statement holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (2) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\); the inductive\nconstruction shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Therefore,\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the non‑negative integers for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the two given requirements exists. ∎"
