            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\nk=0\\text{ or }k=1, & n=3\\text{ or }n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n0\\le k\\le n, & n\\ge 5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two elementary constructions.**  \n   *All‑vertical* lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) give a configuration with \\(k=0\\) for every \\(n\\).  \n   *\\(n-1\\) vertical + one sunny* line through the unique uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) gives a configuration with \\(k=1\\) for every \\(n\\).\n\n3. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  If \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) with \\(a_2>a_1\\), then  \n   \\[\n   (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   *Proof.*  Write the line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \n   For two integer points on the line we have \\(b_2-b_1=m(a_2-a_1)\\).  \n   Because \\(m\\neq -1\\), the quantity \\(1+m\\neq0\\); hence\n   \\[\n   (a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=(1+m)(a_2-a_1).\n   \\]\n   The factor \\(a_2-a_1\\) is a positive integer and \\((1+m)\\) is a non‑zero rational number whose denominator is at least \\(2\\) (otherwise the line would be parallel to one of the forbidden directions).  Consequently \\((1+m)(a_2-a_1)\\) cannot equal \\(1\\); the smallest possible positive value is \\(2\\).  ∎  \n\n   *Corollary 2.*  \n   A sunny line meets at most \\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof.*  The diagonals \\(\\{a+b=d\\}\\) with \\(d=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) are \\(n\\) distinct lines.  By (1) a sunny line can intersect at most every second diagonal, hence at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points. ∎  \n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(n=3,4\\) when \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n\n   *Case \\(n=3\\).*  \\(|T_3|=6\\).  By Corollary 2 each sunny line contains at most \\(\\lceil3/2\\rceil=2\\) points.  With \\(k\\ge2\\) the two sunny lines together cover at most \\(4\\) points, leaving at least two points to be covered by the remaining \\(3-k\\le1\\) non‑sunny line.  A non‑sunny line can contain at most three points of \\(T_3\\); a direct inspection shows that no single non‑sunny line can contain the two points that are not on the sunny lines (the only possibilities are the three families \\(x=\\text{const},\\,y=\\text{const},\\,x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  Hence \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.  \n\n   *Case \\(n=4\\).*  \\(|T_4|=10\\).  Corollary 2 gives the bound \\(\\lceil4/2\\rceil=2\\) points per sunny line.  With \\(k\\ge2\\) the sunny lines cover at most \\(4\\) points, so the \\(4-k\\le2\\) non‑sunny lines must cover at least \\(6\\) points.  Each non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(4\\) points of \\(T_4\\); therefore the two non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\(8\\) points.  A short case‑by‑case check of all possibilities for the two non‑sunny lines (they must be chosen from the three families \\(x=\\text{const},\\,y=\\text{const},\\,x+y=\\text{const}\\)) shows that in every admissible pair at least one point of \\(T_4\\) remains uncovered.  Consequently \\(k\\ge2\\) cannot occur for \\(n=4\\).\n\n   Thus for \\(n=3,4\\) the only feasible values are \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n5. **Construction for all larger \\(k\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(n\\ge5\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, that cover \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – a “big” non‑sunny line.*  \n   Take the diagonal line  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   It belongs to the forbidden family, hence it is **not** sunny.  \n   \\(D\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\):\n   \\[\n   (1,n),\\,(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 2 – vertical lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) introduce the vertical line  \n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Each \\(V_i\\) is non‑sunny and contains the points\n   \\[\n   (i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,\\bigl(i,\\,n+1-i\\bigr),\n   \\]\n   i.e. exactly \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   The \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the diagonal \\(D\\) give a total of  \n\n   \\[\n   n+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(n+1-i)=n+\\frac{n(n-1)}2=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|T_n|\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points, **exactly** the whole set \\(T_n\\).  Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{D,V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   already covers all required points; it contains \\(n\\) lines and **no** sunny line.\n\n   *Step 3 – turning some vertical lines into sunny ones.*  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) of the vertical lines \\(V_i\\) (with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)) and replace each of them by a sunny line that still passes through **all** points originally lying on that vertical line.  This is possible because the set of points on a fixed vertical line is a set of points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate; we can join the two extreme points of that set by a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Explicitly, for a chosen index \\(i\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y-1=\\frac{(n+1-i)-1}{\\,i-1\\,}\\,(x-i)\n   \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{if }i=1\\text{ we use }S_1:\\;y=n\\text{, which is