            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound for a sunny line\n\nWrite a sunny line in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope\n\\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  Choose a primitive integer direction vector\n\\((p,q)\\) (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)) such that \\(m=p/q\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\).\nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) then\n\n\\[\n(a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=t(p,q)\\qquad(t\\in\\mathbb Z\\setminus\\{0\\}),\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=t(p+q). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the line is not parallel to any forbidden direction we have\n\\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0\\) and \\(p+q\\neq0\\).  Moreover \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\); otherwise\n\\(|p+q|=1\\) would force either \\(p=0\\) (horizontal), \\(q=0\\) (vertical) or\n\\(p+q=0\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)), all excluded.\n\nThus \\(|p+q|\\ge2\\) and (1) yields  \n\n\\[\n|(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)|\\ge2 . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn the set \\(T_n\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) can take the consecutive integer\nvalues \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\).  Starting from the smallest value of\n\\(a+b\\) occurring on the line and moving along the line, (2) shows that the\nsum \\(a+b\\) jumps by at least \\(2\\) each time another lattice point of\n\\(T_n\\) is encountered.  Consequently a sunny line meets at most\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  This is recorded as (3) above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points\nof \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n* By (3) the sunny lines together cover at most\n  \\(k\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) points.\n\n* Each non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden\n  directions; therefore it can contain points of at most one *column*\n  (if vertical), one *row* (if horizontal) or one *diagonal*\n  (if of the form \\(x+y=c\\)).  In any case it contains at most \\(n\\)\n  points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of points that can be covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) is\nbounded by\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause all \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points must be covered, (4) must be at\nleast \\(n(n+1)/2\\).  This gives the inequality\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil-n\\Bigr)\\ge-\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\le n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), the factor\n\\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil-n\\) is non‑positive.  The inequality can hold\nonly when \\(k\\le1\\).  Indeed:\n\n* For \\(k=0\\) the left‑hand side of (5) equals \\(n^{2}\\ge n(n+1)/2\\).\n\n* For \\(k=1\\) the left‑hand side equals\n  \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil+(n-1)n\\), which is easily checked to be\n  at least \\(n(n+1)/2\\) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n* For any \\(k\\ge2\\) the left‑hand side of (5) is strictly smaller than\n  \\(n(n+1)/2\\); therefore a covering with \\(k\\ge2\\) sunny lines is\n  impossible.\n\nThus\n\n\\[\nk\\le1\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\n  \\]\n\n  are distinct, none of them is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\)\n  satisfies \\(x=a\\).  Hence all points are covered.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Keep the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The line \\(x=n\\) contains only the point \\((n,1)\\); replace it by any\n  non‑parallel line through that point, for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=-2x+2n+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(L\\) is sunny (its slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)),\n  it contains \\((n,1)\\), and all other points of \\(T_n\\) are still covered\n  by the remaining vertical lines.  The family consists of \\(n\\) distinct\n  lines, exactly one of which is sunny.\n\nBoth values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are therefore realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFrom the impossibility proved in (6) and the explicit constructions for\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) we conclude that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the\nonly possible numbers of sunny lines are\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\text{ or }k=1\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
