 steps one of the coordinates leaves the interval \\([1,n]\\).  Hence\n\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting argument.**  \n   Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n   \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By (3) the sunny lines\n   together cover at most \\(k\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) points.  Every\n   non\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; such a\n   line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a vertical line \\(x=i\\)\n   contains exactly \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points, and the same bound holds for\n   horizontal and diagonal lines).  Hence the total number of points that can be\n   covered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, (4) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Rearranging (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;\\ge\\;k\\Bigl(n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr)\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}2\\;\\ge\\;k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), (6) implies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with (1) we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The three constructions below give the required values \\(k=0,1,2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{ll}\n   k=0:& x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3;\\\\[2mm]\n   k=1:& x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=-2x+7\\;( \\text{sunny});\\\\[2mm]\n   k=2:& x=1,\\;y=x\\;( \\text{sunny}),\\;y=-2x+5\\;( \\text{sunny}).\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(0\\le k\\le n\\!-\\!1\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202f1 \u2013 a mandatory non\u2011sunny line.**  \n     Use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  It covers the whole first\n     column \\(\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202f2 \u2013 reduce to a smaller triangle.**  \n     Delete the first column from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining points form the set\n     \\(T_{n-1}'=\\{(a,b):a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  Translating the coordinates by\n     \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(T_{n-1}'\\) with the original triangle\n     \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\n   - **Step\u202f3 \u2013 apply the induction hypothesis.**  \n     The induction hypothesis guarantees, for the integer \\(k\\) (which satisfies\n     \\(0\\le k\\le (n-1)-1\\) when \\(k\\le n-2\\)), a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) of\n     \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\)\n     sunny lines.  When \\(k=n-1\\) we simply take all \\(n-1\\) lines of\n     \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) to be sunny (the hypothesis allows this because for\n     \\(n-1\\) the maximal admissible number of sunny lines is \\((n-1)-1=n-2\\);\n     the case \\(k=n-1\\) will be handled separately in Step\u202f4).\n\n   - **Step\u202f4 \u2013 the case \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n     Here we need exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines in addition to the vertical line\n     \\(x=1\\).  Consider the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  It passes\n     through exactly one point of each of the remaining columns\n     \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,n\\).  After removing those points, the still uncovered set\n     is the triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\).  By the induction hypothesis applied to\n     \\(n-2\\) we can cover this triangle with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Adding the\n     diagonal line gives a total of \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) sunny lines, as required.\n\n   - **Step\u202f5 \u2013 assembling the configuration.**  \n     The union of the vertical line \\(x=1\\) with the lines supplied by Steps\u202f3\n     (or\u202f4) yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and\n     containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   This completes the induction and shows that every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (8) and the constructive proof above coincide.\n   Hence for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one non\u2011sunny line is necessary  \n\nThe first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  If a line \\(L\\) is sunny, then its direction vector\n\\((p,q)\\) satisfies \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\); consequently \\(L\\) meets the\nvertical line \\(x=1\\) in **at most one** point.  Hence covering all points of\n\\(C_1\\) requires a line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. a non\u2011sunny line.\nTherefore any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains at least one\nnon\u2011sunny line, and\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Choose a primitive integer direction vector\n\\((p,q)\\) of \\(L\\) (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to any\nforbidden direction we have  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) are distinct, then\n\\((a_2-a_1,b_2-b_1)=t(p,q)\\) for some non\u2011zero integer \\(t\\); adding the\ncoordinates gives  \n\n\\[\n(a_2+b_2)-(a_1+b_1)=t(p+q). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus the values of the sum \\(s=a+b\\) taken on \\(L\\cap T_n\\) form an arithmetic\nprogression with step \\(|p+q|\\ge1\\).  Moreover, moving one step along the\nprogression changes the two coordinates by \\((p,q)\\); because both\n\\(|p|\\) and \\(|q|\\) are at least \\(1\\), after at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\) steps one of the coordinates leaves the\ninterval \\([1,n]\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting argument  \n\nAssume a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By (4) the sunny lines\ntogether cover at most \\(k\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) points.  Every\nnon\u2011sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; such a\nline can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a vertical line \\(x=i\\)\ncontains exactly \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points, and the same bound holds for\nhorizontal and diagonal lines).  Hence the total number of points that can be\ncovered by \\(\\mathcal L\\) does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince all \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) points must be covered, (5) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\ge k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\ge1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), (6) implies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (1) we have proved the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-1 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying\n\\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering\n\\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nk=0:& x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3;\\\\[2mm]\nk=1:& x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=-2x+7;\\\\[2mm]\nk=2:& x=1,\\;y=x,\\;y=-2x+5 .\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nAll three families consist of three distinct lines, cover the six points of\n\\(T_3\\), and have the prescribed number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n1. **A compulsory non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   Use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  It covers the whole first\n   column \\(\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n\n2. **Reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   Delete the first column from \\(T_n\\).  The remaining points form  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{n-1}'=\\{(a,b):a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating by \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(T_{n-1}'\\) with the\n   original triangle \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\n3. **Application of the induction hypothesis.**  \n\n   *If \\(k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   The induction hypothesis applied to the integer \\(n-1\\) provides a family\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\) and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Translating the lines back gives\n   \\(n-1\\) lines covering \\(T_{n-1}'\\).  Adding the vertical line \\(x=1\\) we\n   obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *If \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Here we need \\(n-1\\) sunny lines in addition to the vertical line.\n   Introduce the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non\u2011sunny).  It meets each of the\n   remaining columns \\(x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\) in exactly one point, thereby removing\n   one point from each of those columns.  After deleting those points the set\n   that is still uncovered is precisely the triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\).  By the\n   induction hypothesis applied to \\(n-2\\) we can cover \\(T_{n-2}\\) with\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Together with the diagonal line we have\n   \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) sunny lines, and together with the vertical line \\(x=1\\)\n   we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\)\n   and containing exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus in every case a configuration with the required number of sunny lines\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nThe necessary condition (8) and the constructive proof above coincide.\nTherefore for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]"

