)).  \n   - \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines of slope \\(2\\) through the three points,\n     e.g. \\(y=2x-1,\\;y=2x,\\;y=2x-3\\).  \n   All four families consist of distinct lines and each point of \\(T_3\\) is\n   explicitly checked to lie on at least one line, so the base holds.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for all integers smaller than a given \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n   **Step 1 – a mandatory non‑sunny line when needed.**  \n   If \\(k\\le n-1\\) (the case allowed for every \\(n\\)), we use the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny).  It covers the whole first column\n   \\(\\{(1,b):1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n\n   **Step 2 – reduction.**  \n   Removing that column leaves the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n'=\\{(a,b):a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating by \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(T_n'\\) with the triangle\n   \\(T_{n-1}\\).\n\n   **Step 3 – apply the induction hypothesis.**  \n   - If \\(k\\le n-2\\) (i.e. we still need at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines), the\n     induction hypothesis applied to \\(n-1\\) provides a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\)\n     of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n     lines.  Translating these lines back gives \\(n-1\\) lines covering\n     \\(T_n'\\).  Adding the vertical line \\(x=1\\) yields \\(n\\) distinct lines\n     covering all of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   - If \\(k=n-1\\) (possible only when \\(n\\) is odd, because for even \\(n\\) the\n     necessary bound is \\(k\\le n-1\\) and we must have at least one non‑sunny\n     line), we proceed as follows.  Use the diagonal line\n     \\(x+y=n+1\\) (non‑sunny).  This line meets each column \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,n\\)\n     in exactly one point, namely \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  Removing those points leaves\n     precisely the triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\).  By the induction hypothesis applied to\n     \\(n-2\\) we can cover \\(T_{n-2}\\) with \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  Together with\n     the diago