ins exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then\n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (4) for \\(k\\) gives precisely the inequality (1).  Thus (1) is not\n   only necessary but also **sharp**: we have to show that every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can actually be realised.\n\n5. **Inductive construction.**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\ge3\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n   \\]\n\n   contains six points.  The following families satisfy the required\n   specifications (the lines are written in the form “\\(y=mx+b\\)’’; a line\n   parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis is written “\\(x=c\\)’’).\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   k & \\text{family of three lines}\\\\ \\hline\n   0 &\n   \\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n   1 &\n   \\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=2x-5\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n   2 &\n   \\{\\,x=1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   In each case the three lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n   and a direct substitution shows that every point of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one\n   of the listed lines (the verification is elementary and therefore omitted\n   from the table).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for all integers smaller than a given \\(n\\ge4\\).\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   **Step 1 – a mandatory non‑sunny line.**  \n   Take the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0:\\;x=1 .\n   \\]\n\n   It is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column\n   \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n\n   **Step 2 – reduction to a smaller triangle.**  \n   Remove the first column from \\(T_n\\); the remaining set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating by \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(T_n'\\) with the triangle\n   \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).\n\n   **Step 3 – apply the induction hypothesis.**  \n\n   *Case A: \\(k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   By the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) of\n   \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\)\n   sunny lines.  Translating the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) back by\n   \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a+1,b)\\) yields a family of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering\n   \\(T_n'\\) with the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.  Adding the vertical line\n   \\(L_0\\) gives a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole\n   \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Case B: \\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Here we need exactly one non‑sunny line; we keep the vertical line \\(L_0\\) as\n   that line and must cover \\(T_n'\\) with the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines, all of\n   which have to be sunny.  For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define\n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-2). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); because \\(i-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for\n   every \\(i\\) in the range, each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The lines are distinct\n   because they have different slopes.\n\n   **Verification that the lines \\(L_i\\) cover \\(T_n'\\).**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n'\\); thus \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\).  Set  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=b-a+2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  Substituting\n   \\(i\\) into (5) gives\n\n   \\[\n   y = (b-a+1)\\,x-(b-a) .\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y = (b-a+1)a-(b-a)=b,\n   \\]\n\n   so the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).  Hence every point of\n   \\(T_n'\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).\n\n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L_0\\)) is\n   non‑sunny, and it covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  Thus a configuration with\n   \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n   The induction is complete, and we have exhibited a construction for every\n   integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (1) and the explicit constructions for all admissible\n   values together give the exact set of possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n-1\\;}\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one line must be non‑sunny  \n\nThe points of the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nare \\(n\\) distinct points.  \nA sunny line has a finite, non‑zero slope, hence meets the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  Consequently the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) cannot be\ncovered by sunny lines alone; at least one of the \\(n\\) covering lines must be\nparallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. non‑sunny.  Therefore every feasible family of\n\\(n\\) lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Choose a primitive integer direction vector\n\\((p,q)\\) (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\),\n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap T_n\\), then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nThe conditions \\(1\\le a_0+tp\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b_0+tq\\le n\\) give  \n\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting inequality  \n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a vertical line\n\\(x=i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points; the same bound holds for a horizontal\nline and for a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence, if a family \\(\\mathcal L\\)\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines, then\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSolving (4) for \\(k\\) yields precisely the inequality (1).  Thus (1) is a\nnecessary condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive construction  \n\nWe prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following families satisfy the requirements (the lines are distinct,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and a direct substitution shows that every\npoint of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 &\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n1 &\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=2x-5\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n2 &\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the statement true for all integers smaller than a given \\(n\\ge4\\) and\nlet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Step 1.*  Take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nIt is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Step 2.*  Remove that column; the remaining set is  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(T_n'\\) with the triangle\n\\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).\n\n*Step 3.*  Apply the induction hypothesis.\n\n*Case A: \\(k\\le n-2\\).*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) of\n\\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nTranslating the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) back by \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a+1,b)\\)\ngives a family of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n'\\) and still containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Adding \\(L_0\\) yields a configuration of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case B: \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nHere we need precisely one non‑sunny line; we keep \\(L_0\\) as that line and must\ncover \\(T_n'\\) with the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines, all of which must be sunny.\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-2). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); because \\(i-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every\n\\(i\\) in the range, each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the lines are distinct because\ntheir slopes differ.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n'\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a+2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  Substituting this value\nof \\(i\\) into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = (b-a+1)\\,x-(b-a).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\nThus every point of \\(T_n'\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L