            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one line must be non‑sunny  \n\nThe points of the first column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nare \\(n\\) distinct points.  \nA sunny line has a finite, non‑zero slope, hence meets the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  Consequently the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) cannot be\ncovered by sunny lines alone; at least one of the \\(n\\) covering lines must be\nparallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. non‑sunny.  Therefore every feasible family of\n\\(n\\) lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Choose a primitive integer direction vector\n\\((p,q)\\) (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\),\n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap T_n\\), then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\]\n\nThe conditions \\(1\\le a_0+tp\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b_0+tq\\le n\\) give  \n\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting inequality  \n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (a vertical line\n\\(x=i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\le n\\) points; the same bound holds for a horizontal\nline and for a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence, if a family \\(\\mathcal L\\)\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines, then\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSolving (4) for \\(k\\) yields precisely the inequality (1).  Thus (1) is a\nnecessary condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive construction  \n\nWe prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe following families satisfy the requirements (the lines are distinct,\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and a direct substitution shows that every\npoint of \\(T_3\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nk & \\text{family of three lines}\\\\ \\hline\n0 &\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n1 &\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=2x-5\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n2 &\n\\{\\,x=1,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5\\,\\}\\\\[2mm]\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume the statement true for all integers smaller than a given \\(n\\ge4\\) and\nlet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Step 1.*  Take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\nIt is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Step 2.*  Remove that column; the remaining set is  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(T_n'\\) with the triangle\n\\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).\n\n*Step 3.*  Apply the induction hypothesis.\n\n*Case A: \\(k\\le n-2\\).*  \nBy the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) of\n\\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nTranslating the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) back by \\((a,b)\\mapsto(a+1,b)\\)\ngives a family of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n'\\) and still containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Adding \\(L_0\\) yields a configuration of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case B: \\(k=n-1\\).*  \nHere we need precisely one non‑sunny line; we keep \\(L_0\\) as that line and must\ncover \\(T_n'\\) with the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines, all of which must be sunny.\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-2). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); because \\(i-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every\n\\(i\\) in the range, each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the lines are distinct because\ntheir slopes differ.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n'\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a+2 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\).  Substituting this value\nof \\(i\\) into (5) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = (b-a+1)\\,x-(b-a).\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=b\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\nThus every point of \\(T_n'\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_0,L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L_0