re \\(n\\) distinct points.  \n> A sunny line has a finite, non\u2011zero slope, hence meets the vertical line\n> \\(x=1\\) in at most one point. Consequently the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) cannot be\n> covered by sunny lines alone; at least one of the \\(n\\) covering lines must be\n> parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. non\u2011sunny. Therefore every feasible family of\n> \\(n\\) lines satisfies  \n> \\[\n> k\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- \\(C_1\\) indeed contains the points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(b=1,\\dots,n\\).  \n- A sunny line is not vertical, so it intersects \\(x=1\\) in at most one point.  \n- To cover all \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\) we need at least one vertical line.  \n- Hence at least one line is non\u2011sunny, giving \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nAll steps are logically sound. No issue.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Points on a sunny line  \n\n> \u201cLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Choose a primitive integer direction vector\n> \\((p,q)\\) (\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)). Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n> the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\),\n> \\[\n> p\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0. \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> If \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap T_n\\), then all integer points of \\(L\\) are  \n> \\[\n> (a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n> \\]  \n> The conditions \\(1\\le a_0+tp\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b_0+tq\\le n\\) give  \n> \\[\n> |t|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\]  \n> Since \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\),\n> \\[\n> |L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Issue Identified (Justification Gap):**  \nThe derivation of (3) is **not justified**.  \n- From \\(|t|\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\rfloor\\) we obtain at most\n  \\(2\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\rfloor+1\\) integer points, which for\n  \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=1\\) can be as large as \\(2n-1\\), far exceeding\n  \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n- The additional restriction \\(a+b\\le