ine; we keep \\(L_0\\) as that line and must\n> cover \\(T_n'\\) with the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines, all of which must be sunny.\n> For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\; y = (i-1)\\,x - (i-2). \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> The slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i-1\\); because \\(i-1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for every\n> \\(i\\) in the range, each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the lines are distinct because\n> their slopes differ.  \n> **Verification.** Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n'\\). Set \\(i:=b-a+2\\). Since \\(a\\ge2\\) and\n> \\(b\\ge1\\) we have \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\). Substituting this value of \\(i\\) into (5)\n> gives \\(y = (b-a+1)\\,x-(b-a)\\). Evaluating at \\(x=a\\) yields \\(y=b\\); hence\n> \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\). Thus every point of \\(T_n'\\) belongs to\n> exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\).\u201d\n\n**Issues Identified (Critical Errors):**  \n\n1. **Non\u2011sunny line among the supposedly sunny set:**  \n   - For \\(i=1\\) we obtain \\(L_1: y = (0)x - (-1) = 1\\), i.e. the horizontal line\n     \\(y=1\\). Horizontal lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and therefore **not\n     sunny**. Consequently the construction contains **two** non\u2011sunny lines\n     (\\(L_0\\) and \\(L_1\\)), contradicting the requirement that exactly one line\n     be non\u2011sunny.\n\n2. **Incorrect coverage claim:**  \n   - Even ignoring the non\u2011sunny issue, the verification that every \\((a,b)\\in\n     T_n'\\) lies on some \\(L_i\\) is flawed. The assignment \\(i=b-a+2\\) may produce\n     an integer \\(i\\) outside the allowed range or may correspond to a line that\n     does not satisfy the defining equation for that point. A concrete counter\u2011example\n     for \\(n=4\\):  \n     \\(T_4'=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1)\\}\\).  \n     Using the given formula, \\((3,2)\\) yields \\(i=2-3+2=1\\), i.e. \\(L_1: y=1\\),\n     which does **not** contain \\((3,2)\\). Hence the claimed \u201cpoint\u2011by\u2011point