            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary non‑sunny line\n\nThe points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\) are collinear on the vertical line\n\\(x=1\\).  Any sunny line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; therefore it can\nintersect the line \\(x=1\\) in at most one integer point.  Consequently at least\none of the \\(n\\) covering lines must be parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (or,\nby symmetry, to the \\(x\\)-axis or to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  Hence every\nadmissible family contains **exactly one** non‑sunny line and\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points on a sunny line\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its direction vector as \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\),\n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are\n\\((a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq)\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\).  The inequalities\n\\(1\\le a_0+tp\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b_0+tq\\le n\\) give  \n\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\),\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Using (3) for\nthe \\(k\\) sunny lines we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n\\ge|T_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA short calculation shows that this inequality forces \\(k\\le n-1\\) for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\).  Together with (1) we have the necessary condition\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nDefine the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;x=1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_0\\) is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column\n\\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nT_n'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n      =\\{(a-1,b)\\mid (a-1,b)\\in T_{\\,n-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(T_n'\\) is a translate of \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) by the vector \\((1,0)\\).\n\n**Induction basis \\(n=3\\).**  \nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered, for example, by the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=2x-3,\\qquad y=3x-5,\n\\]\n\nwhich contain respectively \\(0,1,2\\) sunny lines; hence the statement holds\nfor \\(k=0,1\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le m-2\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_m\\) and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Translate such a family by \\((1,0)\\);\nthe directions of the lines are unchanged, so the number of sunny lines\nremains \\(k\\).  The translated family now covers \\(T_{m}'\\).\n\nAdding the vertical line \\(L_0\\) yields a family of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(T_{m+1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence for\nevery \\(n\\ge3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required\nconfiguration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for the extremal value \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAgain let \\(L_0:x=1\\) be the unique non‑sunny line.\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) define\n\n\\[\nL_a:\\;y-1=(a-1)(x-a)\\qquad\\text{(equivalently }y=(a-1)x-a(a-2)+1\\text{)}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe direction vector of \\(L_a\\) is \\((1,a-1)\\); because \\(a-1\\neq0\\) and\n\\(1+(a-1)=a\\neq0\\), each \\(L_a\\) is sunny.  The slopes \\(a-1\\) are distinct,\nso the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{L_a\\mid2\\le a\\le n\\}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nWe verify that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*If \\(x=1\\)*, the point belongs to \\(L_0\\).\n\n*If \\(x\\ge2\\)*, write \\(d=y-1\\) (\\(d\\ge0\\)).  \nBecause \\((x,y)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(d\\le n-x\\).\n\n- **Case \\(d=0\\) (i.e. \\(y=1\\)).**  \n  The point \\((x,1)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_x\\) (take \\(a=x\\) in (5)),\n  hence it lies on a sunny line.\n\n- **Case \\(d\\ge1\\).**  \n  Set \\(a=d+1\\) (so \\(2\\le a\\le n\\)).  Substituting \\(a\\) into (5) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y-1=d\\,(x-(d+1)).\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(d=y-1\\), the equality reduces to  \n\n  \\[\n  d = d\\,(x-d-1).\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(d\\neq0\\) we may divide by \\(d\\) and obtain \\(x-d-1=1\\), i.e.  \n\n  \\[\n  x = d+2 = y+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  The inequality \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) together with \\(x=y+1\\) forces\n  \\(y\\le n-1\\); consequently \\(a=d+1=y+1\\) indeed lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n  Hence the point \\((x,y)=(y+1,y)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{y+1}\\) and\n  belongs to the family.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.  The family contains exactly one\nnon‑sunny line (\\(L_0\\)) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (4) together with the explicit constructions of\nSections 4 and 5 shows that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values\nof \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0\\le k\\le n-1 } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
