t at least one line must be parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (or to another forbidden direction) is valid; a sunny line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one integer point, so covering all \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\) forces a non\u2011sunny line.\n\n*Issue:* The conclusion \u201cexactly one non\u2011sunny line\u201d is **unjustified**. The reasoning only shows **at least one** non\u2011sunny line; there is no argument ruling out the possibility of two or more non\u2011sunny lines. Hence the statement is a **Justification Gap**. The derived inequality \\(k\\le n-1\\) does follow from \u201cat least one non\u2011sunny line\u201d, but the claim of uniqueness is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u201cPoints on a sunny line\u201d\n\n> \u201cLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Write its direction vector as \\((p,q)\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\). Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\),\n> \\[\n> p\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p+q\\neq0 .\\tag{2}\n> \\]\n> If \\((a_0,b_0)\\in L\\cap T_n\\) then all integer points of \\(L\\) are \\((a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq)\\;(t\\in\\mathbb Z)\\). The inequalities\n> \\(1\\le a_0+tp\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b_0+tq\\le n\\) give  \n> \\[\n> |t|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\]\n> Since \\(|p|,|q|\\ge1\\),\n> \\[\n> |L\\cap T_n|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Correct parts:* The description of a sunny line\u2019s direction vector and the condition (2) are accurate. The parametrisation of integer points on the line is correct.\n\n*Issue:* From the inequalities \\(1\\le a_0+tp\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b_0+tq\\le n\\) one obtains the bound\n\\[\n|t|\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\nConsequently the number of integer points on the line is at most\n\\[\n2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\]\nIf \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=1\\) this yields up to \\(2n-1\\) points, **n