have \\(k\\ge n\\)**; the maximal possible\n   number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\n   points of \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   The set \\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   - \\(k=0\\): use the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\\).  \n   - \\(k=1\\): take the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\) and the two sunny lines\n     \\(y=2x-3\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)) and \\(y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12\\) (through\n     \\((1,2)\\)).  \n   - \\(k=2\\): use the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\) and the two sunny lines\n     \\(y=x\\) (through \\((1,1)\\)) and \\(y=2x-3\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)).  \n   In each case the three lines are distinct and cover the three points.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n\n   - **If \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n     Take the vertical line  \n     \\[\n     L_0:\\;x=1,\n     \\]  \n     which is non\u2011sunny and covers the whole first column\n     \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \n     Remove this column; the remaining points are exactly the set\n     \\(T_{n-1}\\) translated by the vector \\((1,0)\\):\n     \\[\n     T_n\\setminus\\{x=1\\}= \\{(a+1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{\\,n-1}\\}.\n     \\]\n     By the induction hypothesis there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of\n     \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) and containing exactly\n     \\(k\\) sunny lines (note that \\(k\\le (n-1)-2\\) because \\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \n     Translating every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) by \\((1,0)\\) does not change\n     its direction, hence the number of sunny lines stays \\(k\\).  The\n     translated family now covers the translated set, i.e. the whole\n     \\(T_n\\) except the first column.  Add