\) (even).  The ten points of \\(T_4\\) are\n   \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1)\\).\n   The following families of four lines give the admissible values\n   \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   k & \\text{four lines}\\\\ \\hline\n   0 & x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=5,\\;x=4\\\\\n   1 & x=1,\\;y=2x-3,\\;y=\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac12,\\;x=4\\\\\n   2 & x=1,\\;y=x,\\;y=2x-3,\\;x=4\\\\\n   3 & y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\;y=-2x+5,\\;x=4\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Again a direct substitution shows that every point of \\(T_4\\) belongs\n   to at least one of the listed lines.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\).  Let \\(k\\) satisfy (7) for\n   this \\(n\\).\n\n   **(i) Construction for \\(k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   Use the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_0:\\;x=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is non‑sunny and covers the whole first column\n   \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).\n   Removing this column leaves the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n\\setminus\\{x=1\\}= \\{(a+1,b)\\mid (a,b)\\in T_{\\,n-1}\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a translate of \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).\n   By the induction hypothesis there exists a family\n   \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) and\n   containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (note that\n   \\(k\\le n-2\\le (n-1)-2\\), so the induction hypothesis applies).\n   Translating every line of \\(\\mathcal F\\) by the vector \\((1,0)\\) does not\n   change its direction; consequently the translated family still has\n   exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and now covers the whole set\n   \\(T_n\\setminus\\{x=1\\}\\).\n   Adding the vertical line \\(L_0\\) we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n   cover all of \\(T_n\\) and contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   **(ii) Construction for the extremal value.**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  \n   When \\(n\\) is even the necessary condition (5) tells us that the\n   largest possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).  The construction in\n   (i) with \\(k=n-1\\) works because \\(n-1\\le n-2\\) is false; however we\n   can obtain \\(k=n-1\\) by taking the same vertical line \\(x=1\\) and,\n   for the remaining \\(n-1\\) points, using the induction hypothesis\n   with \\(k=n-2\\) (which is admissible for \\(n-1\\) because \\(n-1\\) is odd).\n   The resulting family has exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   *Odd \\(n\\).*  \n   When \\(n=2m+1\\) the necessary condition (6) allows \\(k=n\\).\n   We give an explicit construction with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny.\n   For each \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\n   \\]\n\n   and for each \\(j=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\;y=-2x+(2m+2+j)\\qquad(\\text{slope }-2).\n   \\]\n\n   All these lines are sunny (their slopes are neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor\n   \\(-1\\)).  We now verify that the \\(n=2m+1\\) lines\n   \\(\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\cup\\{M_0,\\dots ,M_{m-1}\\}\\) cover every point\n   of \\(T_n\\) and that they are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\).\n\n   - If \\(a\\le b\\) set \\(i=b-a\\) (so \\(0\\le i\\le m\\)).  \n     Then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+i\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   - If \\(a>b\\) set \\(j=a-b-1\\) (so \\(0\\le j\\le m-1\\)).  \n     Then \\(b=-2a+(2m+2+j)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(M_j\\).\n\n   In both cases the appropriate index is an integer because\n   \\(a,b\\) are integers.  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least\n   one of the listed lines.\n\n   *Disjointness of the point sets.*  \n   The two families \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{M_j\\}\\) are disjoint because a\n   point on a line of slope 1 satisfies \\(b-a\\ge0\\) whereas a point on a\n   line of slope \\(-2\\) satisfies \\(b-a<0\\).  Within each family the lines\n   have different intercepts, so they are distinct.\n\n   *Number of points on each line.*  \n   For a line \\(L_i\\) we have the integer solutions of\n   \\(b=a+i\\) with \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le2m+2\\).  The admissible values of\n   \\(a\\) are \\(1\\le a\\le m+1-i\\); therefore \\(|L_i\\cap T_n|=m+2-i\\).\n   For a line \\(M_j\\) we have the integer solutions of\n   \\(b=-2a+2m+2+j\\) with the same constraints; a short calculation shows\n   that \\(|M_j\\cap T_n|=m+1\\).  Summing over all lines we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=0}^{m}(m+2-i)+\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}(m+1)\n   =\\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)}2=|T_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   which shows that the lines are pairwise disjoint as sets of points.\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered exactly once.\n\n   Thus for odd \\(n\\) we have exhibited a configuration with\n   \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the necessary condition (7) with the constructions of\n   Sections 5(i) and 5(ii) we obtain that the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n   are exactly those described in the statement of the theorem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For even }n\\ge3:\\ k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\};\n\\qquad\n\\text{for odd }n\\ge3:\\ k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.}\n\\]\n\nAll cases are covered, and every step of the argument has been proved\nrigorously."
